#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fc295d-ebc5-5a8b-8333-139d985dd2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486666} {:answer/id #uuid "46e4ae08-be52-5583-8476-6a01197ebd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486747} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96f4d5-21d0-5dbb-8b99-aac52f757cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486982} {:answer/id #uuid "edca23a3-48cf-59c9-8421-e5dd35d40324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487099} {:answer/id #uuid "f4de4982-fe7b-5fdf-9e7a-721865ec64c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23fc295d-ebc5-5a8b-8333-139d985dd2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486666} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff5d093-dd4c-5ad2-a908-7ac3e6eb6096" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3c08bbb-13dd-5937-9a36-d4dc386d9f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132418" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =3{[4y-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afd5248-63a6-54b0-82c9-25e9a06bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{6-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486616} {:answer/id #uuid "2f40ccc9-c08d-5c80-8bf5-64675b6b4f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486688} {:answer/id #uuid "4c531ca2-1a5c-512b-95e2-ef39648f7a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486758} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebc02a7-5c9e-5d8e-89aa-6707f0d4e2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486951} {:answer/id #uuid "fe72c14f-3623-5add-8ee7-086a6b32d59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{6-y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f40ccc9-c08d-5c80-8bf5-64675b6b4f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486688} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdeecd0-b7a7-5385-bca4-86e41c8c6926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbaf5fee-0009-5eb1-a042-591a8b53019b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132514" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=6-x.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/8c/8c55ebba1610708143086512788e64464e8b50a517257bd63a1c28b89ee7327d.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=6-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=6-x & \\,⟹\\,x=6-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =6-y \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & =(6-y)^{2} \\\\ y & =36-12y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-13y+36 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $y=9$ since it does not satisfy the first equality. Therefore, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/c9/c9c3e3bdec1a007c47d8d1251b790b970a0d367304a5b1e5244bec014e69580d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 6-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b299a6b-4484-532a-9f8c-c14f516cc562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245} {:answer/id #uuid "276d6c64-ff31-5537-9cfc-e67b94462ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266496} {:answer/id #uuid "625522d9-ae80-5048-aff1-7d51ffb91b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267009} {:answer/id #uuid "683122b3-f461-5f8c-9000-a8fdb77f528a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267067} {:answer/id #uuid "77f6ea70-051f-52ad-bfa3-1aafd5a5a61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b299a6b-4484-532a-9f8c-c14f516cc562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245} :answer-field/id #uuid "5098efbb-c619-55aa-9d7a-009fdc05844c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2534dab-da9e-52d9-8510-a8b1311ad552" :question/math-academy-id "q-132529" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}[{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{y}^{3}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc9fc65-1739-5a7c-8405-faa5b206275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324729} {:answer/id #uuid "68b38b5d-aad2-529b-a2d8-79a2b6ddb413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324845} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d2cf74-7d71-5a5b-ac1a-00940077dba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325399} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4c1494-e680-57a5-bec4-f41594ee3b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325450} {:answer/id #uuid "f44c68bb-fac5-5af3-ac88-abac96d890b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc9fc65-1739-5a7c-8405-faa5b206275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324729} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4129ae-aa8f-5600-8743-4d0691281050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89052f85-16e5-5038-8862-a854c23cc74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40596" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d4b5f4-4597-5fb2-8661-87271390504e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486634} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f2da0d-8ced-511f-9af3-03d20168e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486968} {:answer/id #uuid "af82d238-ad19-5d0c-84ab-db2ccd88c886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486987} {:answer/id #uuid "f733835b-7fab-5fb8-8f67-caf01a296a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487122} {:answer/id #uuid "faee4b2e-60b6-5fd3-8b48-4fdb4f844b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f733835b-7fab-5fb8-8f67-caf01a296a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487122} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaed71bb-0e77-5fb5-88f5-a08c5de3d01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e726e2b9-ad90-5a69-bca6-00fc64c93333" :question/math-academy-id "q-89549" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
